ibution. We have our own notes: Candidate A: anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, regarded as a show-off, not open to new ideas. Candidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks. Candidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in a crisis, understands complicated technology, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education. Candidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as a know-it-all, has a hot temper.\n\nWe need to decide our current recommendation. We should consider all candidates, but we haven't discussed A and D yet. We could bring up A or D to ensure coverage. According to the structured notes, we need to still discuss A and D before final recommendation. So we should introduce a candidate not yet discussed, perhaps Candidate D, and compare with current leading candidate (B). Or we could discuss A. Since we need to keep the discussion systematic, we could bring up Candidate D's strengths and concerns, and evaluate if D could be a stronger alternative to B. Or we could bring up Candidate A.\n\nGiven the current leading candidate is B (tied with C). We need to compare with D or A. Let's bring up Candidate D: strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical competence), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). Concerns: regarded as a know-it-all (crew cooperation, communication), has a hot temper (stress resilience, crew cooperation). So D has strong technical and responsibility but concerns about temperament and cooperation.\n\nWe can argue that D's strengths in problem solving and responsibility are valuable for long-distance flights, but his hot temper and know-it-all attitude could jeopardize crew cooperation and calmness under pressure. Compare to B: B has strong reliability and stress handling, but communication issues too. So we need to weigh.\n\nWe could suggest that D might be a viable alternative if his temperament can be managed, but currently B seems stronger overall due to reliability and stress handling, despite communication concerns. However we still need to discuss A later.\n\nThus our contribution: bring up D, compare with B, and maybe adjust recommendation? We could keep vote for B but note D as alternative. Or we could shift to D if we think his strengths outweigh B's concerns. Let's evaluate: B's concerns: pretentious, wrong tone, weak memory for numbers (attention). D's concerns: hot temper, know-it-all (crew cooperation). Both have communication concerns. B's reliability is high (very conscientious). D's responsibility is high but reliability not explicitly mentioned. So B seems stronger.\n\nThus we keep vote for B, but we need to bring D into discussion. So we will say: I want to discuss Candidate D, note his strengths and concerns, and compare with B. I still recommend B, but we need to evaluate D's temperament.\n\nThus public message: mention D's strengths (responsibility, problem solving, concentration, handling unexpected events) and concerns (hot temper, know-it-all). Compare to B's strengths (conscientiousness, stress handling, weather assessment) and concerns (pretentious, wrong tone, weak numeric memory). Conclude that B still appears stronger overall, but we need to consider D further.\n\nNow metadata vote: Candidate B.\n\nProceed."}
